welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wilkes your host and our guest  is alexander evans a counselor in the  British diplomatic service and a senior  fellow at yale's Jackson Institute for  Global Affairs mr. Evans recently was  the Henry Kissinger chair in foreign  policy and international relations at  the Library of Congress and previously  worked at the Department of State as a  senior adviser first to ambassador  Richard Holbrooke and then to ambassador  mark Grossman the US Special  Representative for Afghanistan and  Pakistan he previously served as a  British diplomat in Islamabad and New  Delhi and as a member of the policy  planning staff in London today we talk  with him in a personal capacity about  the u.s. and South Asia welcome mr.  Evans thank you very much for having me  you've done a lot of writing and  research on the US and Pakistan tell us  a little bit about your findings well  it's it's a really complicated  relationship and a relationship in which  both sides feel often betrayed by the  other side and one of the interesting  things about delving into the archives  particularly of u.s. foreign policy on  Pakistan is to see this theme again and  again right back from the late 1950s  onwards an equally if you go back to  Pakistani speeches and press statements  and Pakistani sources you see the same  kind of story about disappointment with  the United States and disappointment  with relationship the reality is  actually relationships been much more  cooperative than people sometimes think  and both sides have tried to use it to  advance their own national interests so  there's a rationality that underpins  both for us approach to Pakistan and  Pakistan's approach to the US the  trouble is V the issues at Pakistan  sort of a relationship at different  times the subjects that the US has  wanted from Pakistan have changed  depending on what the main themes our  fur for those countries at any given  moment okay and what is the traditional  relationship between South Asia  expertise and policymaking in Washington  I think that the myth of a relationship  between expertise and policy making is  that expertise has very little input  into policymaking MDC I'm not sure that  that's true i think the experts regret  sometimes not being able to be at the  top table when key decisions are made  and it is true that when key foreign  policy decisions are made on on india or  pakistan but they're not going to be  made by a mid-level analyst or the  in-house expert on on the subject but at  the same time i think if you look at the  archives the degree to which principles  so the senior members of cabinet in the  US making decisions about foreign policy  actually drawn advice and analysis but  is deeply steeped in knowledge about  Pakistan or India or Afghanistan  actually expertise has a very key role  to play I think the reality though is  that policy decisions are made by  political principles not by experts and  that sometimes is something that makes  experts unhappy do you think that in  making a policy more more should be  given to the expertise the people who  know about you know what is going on  perhaps no I think it's right in any  democratic society right that elected  politicians and members of the cabinet  make four key decisions on national  security for that country there is a  danger about experts which is experts  can think that they are right more than  they actually are right so they can be  overconfident about judgments that they  make all the analysis that they offer so  actually a little too much expertise can  be a dangerous thing in policymaking  resting are there any is there an  example you might be able to cite  perhaps anything spring to mind well you  know none of the experts it seems that  worked on Pakistan in the 1970s  necessarily anticipated the kind of  relationship the US would have with  Pakistan in the 1980s so experts don't  have any more ability to predict the  future than any of us student than your  I do so the danger is when experts think  that they can predict a future the  reality is that none of us can predict  your future right right and given the  current problems in South Asia including  Afghanistan how do you think the US  should be approaching Pakistan I think  the US needs to approach Pakistan with  serious intent and that means even when  there are frustrations recognizing it  there's a relationship there that needs  to be managed with Pakistan it's far  better to raise problems in private than  engage in the sort of megaphone  diplomacy of complaint and that's that's  a problem that sometimes has been  evident both in Islamabad and Washington  the key over the next couple of years is  that the u.s. still needs Pakistan it  needs Pakistan in terms of the US  counterterrorism interests inside  Pakistan it needs Pakistan in terms of  managing any political process in  Afghanistan but will be really the  underpinning of stability or indeed by  lack of stability in South Asia and  becoming five or ten years that doesn't  mean that you give Pakistan everything  that it wants all that you sugarcoat the  relationship but it does mean that you  have to do business and have a working  relationship one of the suggestions  that's been made has been that Pakistan  should be contained or isolated and that  this is a natural argument given the  frustrations of Pakistan in Washington  but i think you know some of the harder  edged arguments about containing  pakistan neglect to understand the  nature that a the US needs pakistan and  be  Pakistan has other friends China Saudi  Arabia Malaysia turkey and actually  isolating Pakistan on the international  scene would be quite difficult to do yes  it does strike me as as we're in a very  tricky situation so to speak because we  do need Pakistan what do you how do you  think the United States should be  working with Pakistan and what is it  exactly do you think that Pakistan wants  from us well in terms of what Pakistan  wants Pakistan or when when we say  Pakistan we're not necessarily talking  here town all of Pakistan's people but  there's there's a sense of wanting to  have a dignity and sovereignty of  Pakistan respected for Pakistan's  military who've been very much V the  architects of Pakistan's national  security culture and interests they're  very keen to balance or at the very  least offset India's growing power in  the world so it's a very india-centric  foreign policy but Pakistan pursues and  this is something that US has been aware  of right back to the late 1950s and  that's a factor that you we have to  understand properly offer Pakistanis and  what they want in terms of how we  actually work with them I think there  has to be a recognition that of where US  and Pakistan's interests combine and one  of those commonalities is in a peaceful  stable Afghanistan and sometimes where  they may differ but Pakistan may want  the u.s. to be much more active for  example on solving the problems between  India and Pakistan and that's not  something at Washington necessarily  wants to get drawn into so it's a case  of taking a balanced approach but is  conscious of the US national interest  and focused on achieving at but is also  conscious of the style of engagement  with Pakistan and the importance of  personal relationships with senior  Pakistanis and the fact that Pakistanis  in particular Pakistan military and  politicians want to be respected  in international affairs and don't want  to be humiliated or perceived to be  humiliated in my relationship with  Washington how do you how would you  characterize our relationships with  certain individuals in Pakistan today I  think it's no it's no secret but the  relationship has been troubled in the  last year and it's been troubled because  Pakistanis have complaints about  Washington's policies both in  Afghanistan and on terrorism and  Washington has complaints about  Pakistan's apparent willingness or  commitment to support us goals in the  region as well so it's been a troubled  relationship and a relationship that has  also been subject to many shocks so some  of the problems in the relationship  don't come from things that either side  can predict but they come from incidents  or crises for the merge with little  notice and can really derail some of the  ordinary business of trying to build a  relationship together can the United  States influence what happens in South  Asia in the coming years I think this  comes to the heart of the question of  diplomacy you know how much influence  can any one country bring to bear on  another and in fact you know the  question of influence is something I  teach in an undergraduate course here on  diplomacy the u.s. clearly does have  some influence in South Asia one case at  the moment is is the crisis that's going  on as we as we speak in in the Maldives  where some form of change in government  has taken place via the former president  argues that it's a cue the new president  argues that or is a constitutional  change and the US has been very active  Bob Lake the Assistant Secretary of  State has flown out to talk to both  sides and the US has clearly played a  positive role in bringing democracy to  the Maldives not something it gets  international headlines in the new york  times but a very important move for the  people of the Maldives it's more  difficult to see India or Pakistan or  Afghanistan choosing to do things that  are not perceived to be in their  national interest because the u.s.  wants them to do that and so you know  India I think will tread very carefully  in terms of being drawn into a an  alliance with the United States visa V  China Pakistan will be very careful not  to be perceived to do Washington's  bidding in Afghanistan but to pursue its  own interest which are much more linked  as i said earlier to india so the limits  of influence i think really come up to  national interest and it's where those  national interests sometimes are  competing rather than points of  cooperation with Washington okay you  mentioned teaching a course on diplomacy  how are you bringing you know what you  have experienced in the outside world in  terms of diplomacy and policy-making  into the into the classroom well I I'm  really enjoying teaching two courses  here yeah 11 is a graduate course on  South Asia and the other is an  undergraduate course on impact and  influence in diplomacy I think with the  South Asia course the approach I've  taken is to try and use declassified  documents wherever I can in the teaching  of a course so if we're going to discuss  the india-pakistan war in 1965 the trick  is actually saying here as you know  here's the CIA analysis here are the  cables that came in from India and  Pakistan from the US embassies here's  the National Security Council document  debating what should be done by the US  about the crisis wow that sounds like  great fun for the further students well  I hope so and I think it offers a window  into how policy actually takes place  yeah and how it relates back to  impressions that countries have of each  other and and how expertise plays in the  system in a way that perhaps just using  secondary sources doesn't give you the  immediacy of a connection to it in terms  of teaching on on diplomacy we all know  that we're living in a in a changing  world a world where new powers like  China and India and Brazil are becoming  more important on the international  scene and I think trying to build a  course around comparative diplomacy so  looking at actually how do these  countries operate in terms of a  diplomatic reporting network how do  make national security policy what's  their approach not just to the US but  their approach to the wider world around  them and I think that's an interesting  exercise in building a global  appreciation of how states operate and  how some multilateral organizations  operate as well so the courses is much  focused on how Singapore or turkey  operate in the international system as  you know the traditional approaches to  diplomacy which tend to be more focused  on you Britain or Germany or V nited  states so for instance Singapore has a  very different approach to diplomacy  than for instance Britain well Singapore  has a different it has a different set  of national interests and it has a  different size of Diplomatic Service the  Singapore Diplomatic Service is much  smaller than the British Foreign Office  and the Singapore foreign services is an  interesting case in point because they  don't actually have an enormous number  of embassies worldwide but what they do  with a very small number of diplomats is  try and build on relationships with  third countries so in terms of their  relationship with Washington or with  Japan or with the European Union they'll  try and use that to really understand as  much as they can about what's going on  in the world and then actually lobby an  influence on Singapore's key national  interests so I think some of that is is  really interesting the way in which they  invest in talent and qualities foreign  service officers is very striking and  the fact that many of those foreign  service officers don't always stay for a  career many will go off and leave and  work for a management consultancy or an  international bank and the Singaporeans  are comfortable with that their argument  is how do they compete for the best  possible talent to pursue diplomatic  excellence in their foreign policy okay  very good thank you so much for being  here today and sharing some of your work  thank you very much for having me for  more information about mr. Evans please  visit our website at yale edu backslash  macmillan report be sure to join us  again for another episode of the  macmillan report made possible through  funding from the Whitney and Betty  Macmillan Center for International and  area studies at Yale  you

